# Task 06 — Nested Subgrid Alignment

**Category:** Layout & Structure
**Difficulty:** near-senior
**Style school:** Neubrutalism

## The problem

Two nested tables (an order and its line items) whose columns must align
perfectly across two levels of nesting, even when the content length and the
number of rows differ between them.

## Constraints

- Must use `subgrid` — column widths cannot be recalculated independently by
  each nested grid.

## Key implementation decisions

- The outer grid defines the real column tracks once:
  `grid-cols-[1fr_80px_100px]` (item / qty / price).
- Each order card spans all 3 outer columns as a single row
  (`col-span-3`), acting as a container for its own line items.
- The inner grid uses `grid-cols-subgrid col-span-3` instead of defining new
  columns of its own — this makes it inherit the exact same 3 column tracks
  from the outer grid, by reference, not by recalculation.
- Order data was deliberately chosen with very different item name lengths
  and a different number of line items per order, so any misalignment would
  be immediately visible if subgrid weren't working correctly.

